  "status": "Refused",
  "description": "My wife and I purchased our first house in October 2024 and with it came the adjoining land on the South East side of our property (92 Grimsdyke Road, Pinner, HA5 4PW) which is adjacent to the pavement running alongside Grimsdyke Road (see title plans, ref.1, attached).\n\nWe would like to extend our garden fence to include the perimeter of this extra land we own (detailed by the blue boundary line in the location plan). As we are responsible for maintaining this land, currently we spend a considerable amount of time cleaning up rubbish and dog excrement left by the general public, and with a newborn child, we are worried about the health and safety issue this could pose. The extension of our garden fence to include the mentioned adjoining land would eliminate the illegal littering issue we face by properly defining the boundaries of our land and greatly increase the area of our garden - utilising the currently unused land. The fence would be the same type of wooden closeboard fence (two meters high) which is currently used around the existing perimeter (see attached photographs). It would be the same natural timber colour to match the surrounding neighbours' fences, and would follow the existing layout of the garden fence of our neighbour's across the road at 41 Wessex Drive.",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12pt&quot;&gt;The proposed boundary fence, given its height and proximity to the adopted highway, would have a detrimental impact on the street scene and openness of public realm in the wider context, contrary to National Planning Policy Framework (2024), Policies GR1 of Harrow Local Plan (2021-2041) and &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height:normal&quot;&gt;adopted Supplementary Planning Document: Residential Design Guide (2010)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;"
  ],
